      "source_groundedness": 0.65,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Count of clinical actions (orders, prescriptions, referrals) is a strong signal for visit length. However the MI follow-up row lists 'continue regimen, repeat lipid panel' yet is scored 0, while other rows with a single order are scored 1, showing inconsistent/under-counting extraction that undermines groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "multi_complaint_flag",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.45,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Multiple presenting complaints is a relevant duration driver. But the flagging is unreliable: single-complaint notes (annual physical, single URI, single knee injury) are flagged 1, while arguably these are single complaints, indicating the flag does not correspond to genuine multi-complaint status."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
  "source_groundedness": 0.68,
  "rationale": "The set mixes generic surface metrics (note_length_chars, sentence_count) offering weak leverage with more query-relevant clinical signals (comorbidity_count, action_count, multi_complaint_flag) that give real axes for modeling visit duration. Usefulness is moderate overall. Groundedness is the weaker dimension: the count/flag columns show over-attribution and inconsistent extraction across the evidence rows (e.g., comorbidity=1 for a clean annual physical, action_count=0 for a note containing orders, multi_complaint_flag=1 for single-complaint visits), pointing to values not always warranted by the source text."
